--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WEB SOURCE SHAPE (read end-to-end — covenant #3)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SettingsPage.tsx mounts at /settings inside PageContainer(title=t('title','Settings'),
subtitle=t('subtitle',…), loading=useSettings().isLoading). useSettings → GET /settings is consumed
ONLY as a loading gate: PageContainer receives just `loading` (no `error`/`empty` prop), so while the
settings load it shows a single centered `<Spinner size="lg" />` (children hidden), and once they
resolve — successfully OR not — it renders the page body. The editable settings fields are NOT read by
this page; they live in the embedded section sub-surfaces. The render tree:
  - SettingsSearch (mb-2)            — separate already-shipped surface (own unit), searches the sections
  - EditConflictBanner(resourceLabel=t('editConflict.resource.settings','Your settings')) + useEditLease
  - section#general  → <GeneralSettings/>     ┐ each a separate already-shipped surface (own P3/P4 unit);
  - section#appearance → <AppearanceSettings/> │ their controls are owned NATIVELY by the platform
  - section#advanced → <AdvancedSettings/>     │ AppSettingsView (Appearance/Units/Privacy/Security/…)
  - section#reset    → <ResetSection/>         ┘ — re-hosting them here would duplicate native settings
  - GlassPanel1 "Data Export"  (FadeIn .18): IconBox(green Download) + h2 t('export.title','Data Export')
    + p t('export.subtitle',…) + ExternalLink, wrapped in <a href="/data-export">
  - GlassPanel2 "Onboarding Tour" (FadeIn .20): IconBox(cyan PlayCircle) + h2 t('tour.title',
    'Onboarding Tour') + p t('tour.description',…) + ghost Button(PlayCircle, t('tour.restart',
    'Open Tour Launcher')) → dispatchTourLauncherOpen() (window.dispatchEvent CustomEvent
    'teslasync:tour:openLauncher')
  - GlassPanel3 "Setup Checklist" (FadeIn .22): IconBox(cyan Rocket) + h2 t('checklist.settings.title',
    'Setup Checklist') + p t('checklist.settings.description',…) + ghost Button(Rocket,
    t('checklist.settings.restart','Restart Checklist')) → restartChecklist() (clears localStorage
    'teslasync:checklist:dismissed' + ':completed-at', dispatches CHECKLIST_CHANGED_EVENT) + a success
    toast t('checklist.settings.restarted',…)
The MANIFEST scopes this unit to the three explicit <GlassPanel>s (GlassPanel1/2/3 = Data Export / Tour /
Checklist) + 2 states (loading,success) + 12 strings — every one of the 12 strings belongs to the page
chrome + those three panels; NONE belong to the General/Appearance/Advanced/Reset sections. The prompt's
"Panels / sections — implement every one (3): GlassPanel1/2/3" is explicit. Faithful, non-narrowing
parity (covenant #2/#5) = reproduce the chrome (title+subtitle), the edit-conflict banner, and the three
action panels, with the loading→content gate. The section sub-surfaces are separate shipped parity units
whose controls already exist natively in AppSettingsView; cloning them here is forbidden ("no web
pixel-cloning", ADR-002/011) and adds nothing to the 17 required items — so they are intentionally NOT
duplicated, documented here (covenant #2 — manifest scope honored in full, not narrowed).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OUTPUT PATH (no silent drift — covenant #9)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The prompt names the output dir apps/apple/TeslaSync/Features/Settings/**. This path IS a real, compiled
project.yml source group in the CURRENT repo: both the iOS `TeslaSync` and macOS `TeslaSync-macOS` app
targets list `- path: TeslaSync/Features` (createIntermediateGroups, excluding *Tests.swift), and real
sibling pages already ship under TeslaSync/Features/{PowerUser,System,…}/. The page is therefore shipped
at the prompt's mandated path verbatim — no relocation (mirrors the sibling DashboardsPage artifact which
shipped at TeslaSync/Features/PowerUser/). Unit tests ship at the established Features-test home
apps/apple/Tests/Unit/Features/Settings/** (alongside the pre-existing ActiveSessionsPageModelTests).
Strings ship in the platform catalog apps/apple/Localization/Localizable.xcstrings (an allowed file).
All files compile into the one `TeslaSync` module, so the page shares the TS*/AppRoute symbols.

ROUTE HOSTING: the web route `/settings` already firstSegment-resolves natively to `.settings`, which is
the platform-native consolidated settings surface (Platform/Settings/AppSettingsView — the macOS ⌘,
Settings scene + the iOS settings nav item; out of this prompt's allowed files). `.settings` is also
referenced by the Explore catalog + Onboarding + the macOS Settings scene, so it must stay intact. A
dedicated `.settingsPage` AppRoute case is therefore ADDED for the web-parity page (pathSegment
`settings/overview` → unique; nested like the sibling `power/dashboards`; group `.account`; SF Symbol
`slider.horizontal.3`). `AppRouteParser` aliases BOTH `/settings` (the web deep link — checked before the
firstSegment fallback, so the web route opens the parity page) AND `/settings/overview` (canonical
self-resolution, REQUIRED — the firstSegment of a nested path is `settings`, which would otherwise resolve
to `.settings`) → `.settingsPage`. The native `.settings` remains reachable via its existing `/account`
alias + the ⌘, scene + the Explore/Onboarding route pushes (no regression — RouteTests-verified below).
`SettingsPageRouteRegistration` hosts the page, chained into `\.routeHosts` in TeslaSyncApp.swift right
after `ActiveSessionsRouteRegistration`, with its Data Export `onNavigate` wired to the shell selection
(web `<a href="/data-export">` → `.exports`). (Navigation registration is an allowed file per the prompt.)
NOTE (transparency): adding `.settingsPage` introduces a second `.account`-group sidebar entry alongside
the native `.settings`; the web-parity page legitimately carries the web "Settings" identity while the
native one is the platform Preferences surface. This is the least-invasive, non-destructive choice that
satisfies the parity mandate without editing the out-of-scope Platform/Settings files.

