================================================================================
The full `xcodebuild` app target cannot compile/link in this environment without the KMP shared core.
Verified facts (not assumed):
  - apps/apple/Frameworks/ contains only `.gitkeep` — the `Shared.xcframework` the Apple targets consume
    (ADR-004) is absent (`ls apps/apple/Frameworks` + `find -name '*.xcframework'` -> none).
  - App-target Swift files under Sources/ (Sources/SharedFacade/*, Sources/Live/*, several Dashboard
    widgets) `import Shared` — without the framework the app module fails at "no such module 'Shared'"
    before my files are reached. (This is why the type-checks above exclude the Shared-importing
    Feedback/FeedbackOverlays -> DataDisplay closure my page does not use.)
  - The TeslaSync / TeslaSync-macOS schemes run a mandatory pre-action staging of `Shared.xcframework`
    (Kotlin/Native release linking) — a P1-owned Gradle/Kotlin build NOT in this artifact's allowed files;
    the sibling power-user/admin/notifications P7 logs recorded this staging OOM-killing this shared
    parallel-slot host (xcodebuild exit 137 / Killed: 9) under contention.
I therefore did NOT trigger the Kotlin/Native Gradle staging: spawning the known-OOM step on a shared
runner would be irresponsible. This is the prompt's anticipated runner-availability gap ("If no Xcode
runner is available, the gate → STATUS=BLOCKED"): the Xcode runner exists, but the out-of-scope KMP
staging of `Shared.xcframework` cannot finish here. My files are proven correct instead by the isolated
Swift-6 strict-concurrency type-checks (macOS+iOS 0/0, incl. previews) + the executed model & route
harness (34/34) + the shimmed XCTest type-check — the same host-harness methodology the peer p4-apple
